 Here's a JavaScript function that sorts a JSON object by its keys in ascending order, with a focus on both being helpful and verbose:

```javascript
function sortJsonObjectByKeys(jsonObject) {
  // First, ensure the input is indeed a JSON object
  if (typeof jsonObject !== 'object' || jsonObject === null) {
    throw new Error('Invalid argument. Please provide a valid JSON object.');
  }

  // Convert JSON object to an array of key-value pairs
  const entries = Object.entries(jsonObject);

  // Use the native sort() method to sort the array based on keys
  entries.sort((a, b) => {
    const keyA = a[0];
    const keyB = b[0];

    if (keyA < keyB) return -1; // Sort ascending
    if (keyA > keyB) return 1;   // ...
    return 0;                  // ...and in case of identical keys, keep the original order
  });

  // Extract sorted keys and values from the sorted entries array
  const sortedKeys = entries.map((entry) => entry[0]);
  const sortedValues = entries.map((entry) => entry[1]);

  // Create a new JSON object using the sorted keys and values
  const sortedJsonObject = Object.fromEntries(entries);

  console.log(`Sorted JSON object keys: ${JSON.stringify(sortedKeys)}`); // Verbosity
  console.log(`Sorted JSON object: ${JSON.stringify(sortedJsonObject)}`); // Verbosity

  return sortedJsonObject;
}
```

This function takes a JSON object as an argument, checks for its validity, converts it into an array of key-value pairs, sorts the array using the `sort()` method, extracts the sorted keys and values, and finally creates a new JSON object using the sorted keys and values. It also logs the sorted keys and the entire sorted JSON object for added verbosity during development.

