 Absolutely! Here's a step-by-step solution to create a Java program that calculates the total expenses for a given list of products:

Step 1: Define a Product class
First, we will define a `Product` class that stores the name of the product and its price.

```java
public class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public Product(String name, double price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }
}
```

Step 2: Create an ArrayList of Products
Next, we will create an `ArrayList` called `products` that will store instances of our `Product` class.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Product> products = new ArrayList<>();

        // Add headphones (5 units @ $50 each)
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            products.add(new Product("Headphones", 50));
        }

        // Add microphones (2 units @ $100 each)
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
            products.add(new Product("Microphone", 100));
        }

        // Add speakers (3 units @ $150 each)
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            products.add(new Product("Speaker", 150));
        }
    }
}
```

Step 3: Calculate Total Expenses
Now, we will calculate the total expenses by iterating through the `ArrayList` and summing up the prices.

```java
// Calculate the total expenses
double totalExpenses = 0;
for (Product p : products) {
    totalExpenses += p.getPrice();
}

System.out.println("Total Expenses: $" + totalExpenses);
